#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:1
msgid "Documentation tests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:3
msgid ""
"`rustdoc` supports executing your documentation examples as tests. This "
"makes sure that examples within your documentation are up to date and "
"working."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:6
msgid "The basic idea is this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:9
msgid ""
"/// # Examples\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```\n"
"/// let x = 5;\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:17
msgid ""
"Here, the triple backticks start and end the code block. If this were in a "
"file named `foo.rs`, running `rustdoc --test foo.rs` will extract this "
"example, and then run it as a test."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:21
msgid ""
"Please note that by default, if no language is set for the block code, "
"rustdoc assumes it is Rust code. So the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:31
msgid "is strictly equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:39
msgid "There's some subtlety though! Read on for more details."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:41
msgid "Passing or failing a doctest"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:43
msgid ""
"Like regular unit tests, regular doctests are considered to \"pass\" if they "
"compile and run without panicking. So if you want to demonstrate that some "
"computation gives a certain result, the `assert!` family of macros works the "
"same as other Rust code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:49
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:50
msgid "\"foo\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:53
msgid ""
"This way, if the computation ever returns something different, the code "
"panics and the doctest fails."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:56
msgid "Pre-processing examples"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:58
msgid ""
"In the example above, you'll note something strange: there's no `main` "
"function! Forcing you to write `main` for every example, no matter how "
"small, adds friction and clutters the output. So `rustdoc` processes your "
"examples slightly before running them. Here's the full algorithm `rustdoc` "
"uses to preprocess examples:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:64
msgid ""
"Some common `allow` attributes are inserted, including `unused_variables`, "
"`unused_assignments`, `unused_mut`, `unused_attributes`, and `dead_code`. "
"Small examples often trigger these lints."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:68
msgid "Any attributes specified with `#![doc(test(attr(...)))]` are added."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:69
msgid "Any leading `#![foo]` attributes are left intact as crate attributes."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:70
msgid ""
"If the example does not contain `extern crate`, and `#!"
"[doc(test(no_crate_inject))]` was not specified, then `extern crate "
"<mycrate>;` is inserted (note the lack of `#[macro_use]`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:73
msgid ""
"Finally, if the example does not contain `fn main`, the remainder of the "
"text is wrapped in `fn main() { your_code }`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:76
msgid "For more about that caveat in rule 4, see \"Documenting Macros\" below."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:78
msgid "Hiding portions of the example"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:80
msgid ""
"Sometimes, you need some setup code, or other things that would distract "
"from your example, but are important to make the tests work. Consider an "
"example block that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:85
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// /// Some documentation.\n"
"/// # fn foo() {} // this function will be hidden\n"
"/// println!(\"Hello, World!\");\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:93
msgid "It will render like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:96
msgid "/// Some documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:98
msgid "\"Hello, World!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:101
msgid ""
"Yes, that's right: you can add lines that start with `# `, and they will be "
"hidden from the output, but will be used when compiling your code. You can "
"use this to your advantage. In this case, documentation comments need to "
"apply to some kind of function, so if I want to show you just a "
"documentation comment, I need to add a little function definition below it. "
"At the same time, it's only there to satisfy the compiler, so hiding it "
"makes the example more clear. You can use this technique to explain longer "
"examples in detail, while still preserving the testability of your "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:111
msgid "For example, imagine that we wanted to document this code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:116
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:126
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:134
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:142
msgid "\"{}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:119
msgid "We might want the documentation to end up looking like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:121
msgid "First, we set `x` to five:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:129
msgid "Next, we set `y` to six:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:137
msgid "Finally, we print the sum of `x` and `y`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:145
msgid ""
"To keep each code block testable, we want the whole program in each block, "
"but we don't want the reader to see every line every time.  Here's what we "
"put in our source code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:175
msgid ""
"By repeating all parts of the example, you can ensure that your example "
"still compiles, while only showing the parts that are relevant to that part "
"of your explanation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:179
msgid ""
"The `#`\\-hiding of lines can be prevented by using two consecutive hashes "
"`##`. This only needs to be done with the first `#` which would've otherwise "
"caused hiding. If we have a string literal like the following, which has a "
"line that starts with a `#`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:185
msgid ""
"\"foo\n"
"## bar # baz\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:189
msgid "We can document it by escaping the initial `#`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:191
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"/// let s = \"foo\n"
"/// ## bar # baz\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:196
msgid ""
"Here is an example with a macro rule which matches on tokens starting with "
"`#`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:199
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// macro_rules! ignore { (##tag) => {}; }\n"
"/// ignore! {\n"
"///     ###tag\n"
"/// }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:208
msgid ""
"As you can see, the rule is expecting two `#`, so when calling it, we need "
"to add an extra `#` because the first one is used as escape."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:211
msgid "Using `?` in doc tests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:213
msgid ""
"When writing an example, it is rarely useful to include a complete error "
"handling, as it would add significant amounts of boilerplate code. Instead, "
"you may want the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:218
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"/// let mut input = String::new();\n"
"/// io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:226
msgid ""
"The problem is that `?` returns a `Result<T, E>` and test functions don't "
"return anything, so this will give a mismatched types error."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:229
msgid ""
"You can get around this limitation by manually adding a `main` that returns "
"`Result<T, E>`, because `Result<T, E>` implements the `Termination` trait:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:233
msgid ""
"/// A doc test using ?\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"///\n"
"/// fn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n"
"///     let mut input = String::new();\n"
"///     io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"///     Ok(())\n"
"/// }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:247
msgid ""
"Together with the `# ` from the section above, you arrive at a solution that "
"appears to the reader as the initial idea but works with doc tests:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:251
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"/// # fn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n"
"/// let mut input = String::new();\n"
"/// io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"/// # Ok(())\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:262
msgid ""
"As of version 1.34.0, one can also omit the `fn main()`, but you will have "
"to disambiguate the error type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:266
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"/// let mut input = String::new();\n"
"/// io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"/// # Ok::<(), io::Error>(())\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:275
msgid ""
"This is an unfortunate consequence of the `?` operator adding an implicit "
"conversion, so type inference fails because the type is not unique. Please "
"note that you must write the `(())` in one sequence without intermediate "
"whitespace so that `rustdoc` understands you want an implicit `Result`\\-"
"returning function."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:280
msgid "Showing warnings in doctests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:282
msgid ""
"You can show warnings in doctests by running `rustdoc --test --test-args=--"
"show-output` (or, if you're using cargo, `cargo test --doc -- --show-"
"output`). By default, this will still hide `unused` warnings, since so many "
"examples use private functions; you can add `#![warn(unused)]` to the top of "
"your example if you want to see unused variables or dead code warnings. You "
"can also use [`#![doc(test(attr(warn(unused))))]`](the-doc-attribute."
"md#testattr) in the crate root to enable warnings globally."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:290
msgid "Documenting macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:292
msgid "Here’s an example of documenting a macro:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:295
msgid ""
"/// Panic with a given message unless an expression evaluates to true.\n"
"///\n"
"/// # Examples\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```\n"
"/// # #[macro_use] extern crate foo;\n"
"/// # fn main() {\n"
"/// panic_unless!(1 + 1 == 2, “Math is broken.”);\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```should_panic\n"
"/// # #[macro_use] extern crate foo;\n"
"/// # fn main() {\n"
"/// panic_unless!(true == false, “I’m broken.”);\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:319
msgid ""
"You’ll note three things: we need to add our own `extern crate` line, so "
"that we can add the `#[macro_use]` attribute. Second, we’ll need to add our "
"own `main()` as well (for reasons discussed above). Finally, a judicious use "
"of `#` to comment out those two things, so they don’t show up in the output."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:324
msgid "Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:326
msgid ""
"Code blocks can be annotated with attributes that tell `rustdoc` how to "
"build and interpret the test. They follow the [code fence](https://spec."
"commonmark.org/0.29/#code-fence) in the opening line. As such, they share "
"the place with language strings like `rust` or `text`. Multiple attributes "
"can be provided by separating them with commas, spaces or tabs. You can also "
"write comments which are enclosed in parentheses `(…)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:332
msgid ""
"As alluded to in the introduction at the very top, unless you specify `rust` "
"or something that isn't an attribute (except for `custom`), the code block "
"is assumed to be Rust source code (and is syntax highlighted as such)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:336
msgid ""
"You can of course add `rust` explicitly (like `rust,ignore`) if the Markdown "
"is also consumed by other tools (e.g., if it's contained inside of a `README."
"md` that's included via `include_str`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:339
msgid "`ignore`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:341
msgid ""
"The `ignore` attribute tells `rustdoc` to ignore your code. This is useful "
"if you would like to have Rust syntax highlighting but the snippet is "
"incomplete or pseudocode. It is customary to add the reason why it should be "
"ignored in a `(…)` comment."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:346
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore\n"
"/// fn foo() {\n"
"/// ```\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```ignore (needs extra dependency)\n"
"/// use dependency::functionality;\n"
"/// functionality();\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:357
msgid ""
"Do note that this is almost never what you want as it's the most generic. "
"Instead, consider annotating it with `text` if it's not code or using `#`s "
"to get a working example that only shows the part you care about."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:361
msgid "`should_panic`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:363
msgid ""
"`should_panic` tells `rustdoc` that the code should compile correctly but "
"panic during execution. If the code doesn't panic, the test will fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:367
msgid ""
"/// ```should_panic\n"
"/// assert!(false);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:373
msgid "`no_run`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:375
msgid ""
"The `no_run` attribute will compile your code but not run it. This is "
"important for examples such as \"Here's how to retrieve a web page,\" which "
"you would want to ensure compiles, but might be run in a test environment "
"that has no network access. This attribute can also be used to demonstrate "
"code snippets that can cause Undefined Behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:382
msgid ""
"/// ```no_run\n"
"/// loop {\n"
"///     println!(\"Hello, world\");\n"
"/// }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:390
msgid "`compile_fail`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:392
msgid ""
"`compile_fail` tells `rustdoc` that the compilation should fail. If it "
"compiles, then the test will fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:396
msgid ""
"/// ```compile_fail\n"
"/// let x = 5;\n"
"/// x += 2; // shouldn't compile!\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:408
msgid "`edition…`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:410
msgid ""
"`edition2015`, `edition2018`, `edition2021`, and `edition2024` tell "
"`rustdoc` that the code sample should be compiled using the respective "
"edition of Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:414
msgid ""
"/// Only runs on the 2018 edition.\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```edition2018\n"
"/// let result: Result<i32, ParseIntError> = try {\n"
"///     \"1\".parse::<i32>()?\n"
"///         + \"2\".parse::<i32>()?\n"
"///         + \"3\".parse::<i32>()?\n"
"/// };\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:426
msgid "`standalone_crate`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:428
msgid ""
"Starting in the 2024 edition[^edition-note], compatible doctests are merged "
"as one before being run. We combine doctests for performance reasons: the "
"slowest part of doctests is to compile them. Merging all of them into one "
"file and compiling this new file, then running the doctests is much faster. "
"Whether doctests are merged or not, they are run in their own process."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:433
msgid "An example of time spent when running doctests:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:435
msgid "[sysinfo crate](https://crates.io/crates/sysinfo):"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:443
msgid "Rust core library:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:451
msgid ""
"This is based on the edition of the whole crate, not the edition of the "
"individual test case that may be specified in its code attribute."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:454
msgid "In some cases, doctests cannot be merged. For example, if you have:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:457
msgid ""
"//! ```\n"
"//! let location = std::panic::Location::caller();\n"
"//! assert_eq!(location.line(), 4);\n"
"//! ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:463
msgid ""
"The problem with this code is that, if you change any other doctests, it'll "
"likely break when running `rustdoc --test`, making it tricky to maintain."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:466
msgid ""
"This is where the `standalone_crate` attribute comes in: it tells `rustdoc` "
"that a doctest should not be merged with the others. So the previous code "
"should use it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:470
msgid ""
"//! ```standalone_crate\n"
"//! let location = std::panic::Location::caller();\n"
"//! assert_eq!(location.line(), 4);\n"
"//! ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:476
msgid ""
"In this case, it means that the line information will not change if you add/"
"remove other doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:479
msgid "`ignore-…`: Ignoring targets"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:481
msgid ""
"Attributes starting with `ignore-` can be used to ignore doctests for "
"specific targets. For example, `ignore-x86_64` will avoid building doctests "
"when the target name contains `x86_64`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:486
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore-x86_64\n"
"/// assert!(2 == 2);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:492
msgid ""
"This doctest will not be built for targets such as `x86_64-unknown-linux-"
"gnu`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:494
msgid "Multiple ignore attributes can be specified to ignore multiple targets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:497
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore-x86_64,ignore-windows\n"
"/// assert!(2 == 2);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:503
msgid ""
"If you want to preserve backwards compatibility for older versions of "
"rustdoc, you can specify both `ignore` and `ignore-`, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:507
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore,ignore-x86_64\n"
"/// assert!(2 == 2);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:513
msgid ""
"In older versions, this will be ignored on all targets, but starting with "
"version 1.88.0, `ignore-x86_64` will override `ignore`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:516
msgid "`{…}` & `custom`: Custom CSS classes for code blocks"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:519
msgid ""
"/// ```custom,{class=language-c}\n"
"/// int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:525
msgid ""
"The text `int main(void) { return 0; }` is rendered without highlighting in "
"a code block with the class `language-c`. This can be used to highlight "
"other languages through JavaScript libraries for example."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:529
msgid ""
"Without the `custom` attribute, it would be generated as a Rust code example "
"with an additional `language-C` CSS class. Therefore, if you specifically "
"don't want it to be a Rust code example, don't forget to add the `custom` "
"attribute."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:533
msgid ""
"To be noted that you can replace `class=` with `.` to achieve the same "
"result:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:536
msgid ""
"/// ```custom,{.language-c}\n"
"/// int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:542
msgid ""
"To be noted, `rust` and `{.rust}` / `{class=rust}` have different effects: "
"`rust` indicates that this is a Rust code block whereas the two others add a "
"\"rust\" CSS class on the code block in the generated HTML."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:546
msgid "You can also use double quotes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:549
msgid ""
"/// ```\"not rust\" {.\"hello everyone\"}\n"
"/// int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:555
msgid "`test_harness`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:557
msgid ""
"With `test_harness` applied, `rustdoc` will run any contained _test "
"functions_ instead of the (potentially implicit) `main` function."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:561
msgid ""
"//! ```test_harness\n"
"//! #[test]\n"
"//! #[should_panic]\n"
"//! fn abc() { assert!(false); }\n"
"//!\n"
"//! #[test]\n"
"//! fn xyz() { assert!(true); }\n"
"//! ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:571
msgid ""
"You can read more about _test functions_ in [the Book](../../book/ch11-01-"
"writing-tests.html) or in [the Rust Reference](../../reference/attributes/"
"testing.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:576
msgid "Syntax reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:578
msgid ""
"The _exact_ syntax for code blocks, including the edge cases, can be found "
"in the [Fenced Code Blocks](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.29/#fenced-code-"
"blocks) section of the CommonMark specification."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:581
msgid ""
"Rustdoc also accepts _indented_ code blocks as an alternative to fenced code "
"blocks: Instead of surrounding your code with a [code fence](https://spec."
"commonmark.org/0.29/#code-fence) (e.g., three backticks), you can indent "
"each line by four or more spaces."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:590
msgid ""
"These, too, are documented in the CommonMark specification, in the [Indented "
"Code Blocks](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.29/#indented-code-blocks) section."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:592
msgid ""
"However, it's preferable to use fenced code blocks over indented code "
"blocks. Not only are fenced code blocks considered more idiomatic for Rust "
"code, but there is no way to use attributes such as `ignore` or "
"`should_panic` with indented code blocks."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:597
msgid "Include items only when collecting doctests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:599
msgid ""
"Rustdoc's documentation tests can do some things that regular unit tests "
"can't, so it can sometimes be useful to extend your doctests with samples "
"that wouldn't otherwise need to be in documentation. To this end, Rustdoc "
"allows you to have certain items only appear when it's collecting doctests, "
"so you can utilize doctest functionality without forcing the test to appear "
"in docs, or to find an arbitrary private item to include it on."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:605
msgid ""
"When compiling a crate for use in doctests (with `--test` option), `rustdoc` "
"will set `#[cfg(doctest)]`. Note that they will still link against only the "
"public items of your crate; if you need to test private items, you need to "
"write a unit test."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:609
msgid ""
"In this example, we're adding doctests that we know won't compile, to verify "
"that our struct can only take in valid data:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:613
msgid ""
"/// We have a struct here. Remember it doesn't accept negative numbers!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:615
msgid ""
"/// ```compile_fail\n"
"/// let x = my_crate::MyStruct(-5);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:623
msgid ""
"Note that the struct `MyStructOnlyTakesUsize` here isn't actually part of "
"your public crate API. The use of `#[cfg(doctest)]` makes sure that this "
"struct only exists while `rustdoc` is collecting doctests. This means that "
"its doctest is executed when `--test` is passed to rustdoc, but is hidden "
"from the public documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:628
msgid ""
"Another possible use of `#[cfg(doctest)]` is to test doctests that are "
"included in your README file without including it in your main "
"documentation. For example, you could write this into your `lib.rs` to test "
"your README as part of your doctests:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:633
msgid "\"../README.md\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:638
msgid ""
"This will include your README as documentation on the hidden struct "
"`ReadmeDoctests`, which will then be tested alongside the rest of your "
"doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:641
msgid "Controlling the compilation and run directories"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:643
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc --test` will compile and run documentation test "
"examples from the same working directory. The compilation directory is being "
"used for compiler diagnostics, the `file!()` macro and the output of "
"`rustdoc` test runner itself, whereas the run directory has an influence on "
"file-system operations within documentation test examples, such as `std::fs::"
"read_to_string`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:649
msgid ""
"The `--test-run-directory` flag allows controlling the run directory "
"separately from the compilation directory. This is particularly useful in "
"workspaces, where compiler invocations and thus diagnostics should be "
"relative to the workspace directory, but documentation test examples should "
"run relative to the crate directory."
msgstr ""
